"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:06,640\nThe amygdala: why emotion sticks\n\n3\n00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:12,171\nIn 1994, Larry Cahill,\n\n4\n00:00:12,365 --> 00:00:16,706\nJames McGaugh and their colleagues showed people a slide show with a story.\n\n5\n00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:19,974\nFor half of them,\n\n6\n00:00:20,343 --> 00:00:23,015\nthe middle of the story was upsetting: a boy is badly hurt in a car accident.\n\n7\n00:00:23,559 --> 00:00:27,445\nA week later, those people remembered that part especially well.\n\n8\n00:00:27,898 --> 00:00:29,402\nA drug called propranolol,\n\n9\n00:00:29,422 --> 00:00:31,913\nwhich blocks some effects of adrenaline,\n\n10\n00:00:32,154 --> 00:00:33,834\ntook that advantage away.\n\n11\n00:00:34,004 --> 00:00:36,435\nThe reason lies here,\n\n12\n00:00:36,824 --> 00:00:39,261\nin the amygdala: two almond-shaped clusters of cells,\n\n13\n00:00:39,514 --> 00:00:41,704\none deep in each temporal lobe,\n\n14\n00:00:41,828 --> 00:00:43,754\njust in front of the hippocampus.\n\n15\n00:00:45,924 --> 00:00:47,290\nWhen something stirs you,\n\n16\n00:00:47,530 --> 00:00:50,424\nstress hormones such as adrenaline rise.\n\n17\n00:00:50,829 --> 00:00:56,497\nThe amygdala responds, and signals the hippocampus and other regions to strengthen the memory.\n\n18\n00:00:57,061 --> 00:01:01,241\nEmotional events get a boost while the memory is still settling in.\n\n19\n00:01:02,911 --> 00:01:05,495\nThe amygdala also learns what to fear.\n\n20\n00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:11,474\nIn 1995, researchers in Iowa paired a color with a loud noise.\n\n21\n00:01:11,858 --> 00:01:16,674\nA patient with damage to both amygdalae knew which color came before the noise,\n\n22\n00:01:16,926 --> 00:01:18,820\nbut her body never reacted to it.\n\n23\n00:01:19,308 --> 00:01:22,542\nA patient with damage to the hippocampus did react,\n\n24\n00:01:22,715 --> 00:01:23,961\nbut couldn't say why.\n\n25\n00:01:26,631 --> 00:01:28,678\nEmotion also narrows memory.\n\n26\n00:01:29,148 --> 00:01:31,162\nIn a 1987 study,\n\n27\n00:01:31,354 --> 00:01:35,180\npeople who saw a gun in a slide show kept looking at the gun,\n\n28\n00:01:35,423 --> 00:01:37,862\nand were worse at picking out the man holding it.\n\n29\n00:01:38,368 --> 00:01:40,833\nAnd memories of shocking news feel vivid,\n\n30\n00:01:41,123 --> 00:01:42,817\nbut after September eleventh,\n\n31\n00:01:42,832 --> 00:01:45,493\nthey changed as much as everyday memories.\n\n32\n00:01:45,907 --> 00:01:47,806\nOnly confidence stayed high.\n\n33\n00:01:49,476 --> 00:01:51,128\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n34\n00:01:51,485 --> 00:01:54,064\nEmotion is a strong signal to remember.\n\n35\n00:01:54,415 --> 00:01:59,004\nCuriosity, surprise and stories that matter can help lessons stick.\n\n36\n00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:03,266\nBut fear narrows attention and weakens the prefrontal workbench.\n\n37\n00:02:03,669 --> 00:02:05,663\nAim for interest, not fear.\n\n38\n00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:09,529\nThe amygdala tags what matters.\n\n39\n00:02:09,746 --> 00:02:12,701\nIt tells the rest of the brain: remember this.\n\n"
